      "title": "INTRODUCTION",
      "kind": "intro",
      "page": 0,
      "text_preview": "D IFFERENT kinds of molecules can be detected in a biological sample by measuring the light intensity emitted by a chemoluminant reaction. In that way, an infection of a patient by a specific virus (such as HIV) can be identified by detecting the presence of antibodies in the blood. Use of the photometric measurement of a chemoluminant reaction may lead to the development of inexpensive yet precis ..."
    },
    {
      "number": "II",
      "title": "CIRCUIT DESIGN AND OPERATION",
      "kind": "design",
      "page": 0,
      "text_preview": "The architecture is shown in Fig. 2. The sensor consists of a photodiode, a current buffer, and a sigma–delta modulator. Photocurrent produced by the photodiode is buffered by the current buffer into an integrating capacitor. In order to improve the photocurrent-to-voltage conversion efficiency, the photocurrent is integrated on the external integrating capacitor rather than on the diode junction  ..."
    },
    {
      "number": "III",
      "title": "REALIZATION",
      "kind": "design",
      "page": 3,
      "text_preview": "The chip was fabricated using HP 0.5m CMOS process through MOSIS services. The process has a single poly layer, three metal layers (only two were used for the design; the third provided a light shield for electronic components), and linear capacitors. The power supply is a single 3.3-V source. Chip parameters are outlined in Table I. The die photograph is presented in Fig. 11. There are two sensor ..."
    },
    {
      "number": "IV",
      "title": "EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS",
      "kind": "results",
      "page": 3,
      "text_preview": "A pulse width modulated LED was employed to illuminate the sensor, providing for relative intensity optoelectric measurements. In contrast to amplitude modulation of continuous light, pulse width can be controlled accurately over multiple orders of magnitude, providing for wider dynamic range. A 1-kHz pulse rate is modulated by a low-frequency (1–3 Hz) sine wave. Thus, the input contains the desir ..."
    },
    {
      "number": "V",
      "title": "CONCLUSION",
      "kind": "conclusion",
      "page": 5,
      "text_preview": "We have described a low-light-level CMOS sensor and preprocessor for a disposable medical probe. The probe is designed IEEE JOURNAL OF SOLID-STATE CIRCUITS, VOL. 36, NO. 10, OCTOBER 2001 for chemoluminant diagnostic applications, where the light signal is low bandwidth (10 Hz) and does not exceed 4 A/cm in the visible spectrum. A single-bit first-order sigma–delta modulator has been employed for a ..."
